The settlement of [[Las Vegas]], [[Nevada]] was founded in 1905 before the opening of a [[Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad|railroad that linked Los Angeles and Salt Lake City]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Early Las Vegas {{!}} American Experience {{!}} PBS |url=https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/lasvegas-early/ |access-date=2025-06-12 |website=www.pbs.org |language=en}}</ref> The stopover attracted some farmers (mostly from Utah) to the area, and fresh water was irrigated to the settlement.{{cn|date=June 2025}} In 1911, the town was incorporated as part of the newly founded [[Clark County, Nevada|Clark County]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Clark County Cultural Overview |url=https://extension.unr.edu/publication.aspx?PubID=4445 |access-date=2025-06-12 |website=Extension {{!}} University of Nevada, Reno |language=en-us}}</ref> Urbanization took off in 1931 when work started on the Boulder Dam (now the [[Hoover Dam]]), bringing a huge influx of young male workers, for whom theaters and [[casino]]s were built, largely by the [[Mafia]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Little |first=Becky |date=2022-05-13 |title=How Las Vegas Became a Gambling Mecca |url=https://www.history.com/articles/las-vegas-history-mobsters-gambling |access-date=2025-06-12 |website=HISTORY |language=en}}</ref> Electricity from the dam also enabled the building of many new hotels along the Strip. The arrival of [[Howard Hughes]] in 1966 did much to offset mob influence and helped turn Las Vegas into more of a family tourist center.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Las Vegas – Oldroyd Photography |url=https://oldroydphotography.com/cities/las-vegas/ |access-date=2025-06-12 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-09-16 |title=Las Venturas |url=https://gtastarsandstripes.miraheze.org/wiki/Las_Venturas |access-date=2025-06-12 |website=GTA: Stars & Stripes Wiki |language=en}}</ref>

The name Las Vegas—Spanish for "the meadows"—was given to the area in 1829 by Rafael Rivera, a member of the Spanish explorer [[Antonio Armijo]]’s trading party that was traveling to [[Los Angeles, California|Los Angeles]], and stopped for water there on the [[Old Spanish Trail (trade route)|Old Spanish Trail]] from [[New Mexico]]. At that time, several parts of the valley contained [[Artesian aquifer|artesian wells]] surrounded by extensive green areas. The flows from the wells fed the [[Las Vegas Wash]], which runs to the [[Colorado River]].

