[[File:Caltech-Submillimeter-Observatory (straightened).jpg|thumb|The [[Caltech Submillimeter Observatory]] at [[Mauna Kea Observatory]] was commissioned in 1988, and had a 10.4 m (34 ft) dish]]

'''Submillimetre astronomy''' or '''submillimeter astronomy''' (see [[spelling differences]]) is the branch of [[observational astronomy]] that is conducted at submillimetre wavelengths (i.e., [[terahertz radiation]]) of the [[electromagnetic spectrum]]. Astronomers place the submillimetre waveband between the [[far-infrared]] and [[microwave]] wavebands, typically taken to be between a few hundred [[micrometre]]s and a [[millimetre]]. It is still common in submillimetre astronomy to quote wavelengths in 'microns', the old name for micrometre.

Submillimetre observations can be used to trace [[Emission spectrum|emission]] from gas and dust, including the CI, CO, and CII lines.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |url=https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/12982/chapter/13#441 |title=Read "Panel Reports—New Worlds, New Horizons in Astronomy and Astrophysics" at NAP.edu |date=2011 |doi=10.17226/12982 |isbn=978-0-309-15962-3 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Submillimeter Galaxies - A.W. Blain et al. |url=https://ned.ipac.caltech.edu/level5/Sept04/Blain/Blain_contents.html |access-date=2025-02-15 |website=ned.ipac.caltech.edu}}</ref> Sources behind this emission include [[molecular cloud]]s and [[Dark nebula|dark cloud]] cores, which can be used to clarify the process of star formation from earliest [[Gravitational collapse|collapse]] to [[Stellar birthline|stellar birth]], by determining [[Astrochemistry|chemical abundances]] in dark clouds and the [[Interstellar medium|cooling mechanisms]] for the molecules which [[List of interstellar and circumstellar molecules|comprise them]]. Other sources include [[Protoplanetary disk|protoplanetary discs]], [[Starburst galaxy|dusty starburst galaxies]] in the early Universe, immediate environments surrounding [[Active galactic nucleus|AGN]], and secondary [[Anisotropy|anisotropies]] in the [[cosmic microwave background]].<ref name=":0" /><ref name="ReferenceA">{{Citation |last1=Scott |first1=Douglas |title=Cosmology in front of the background: Studying the growth of structure at CMB wavelengths |date=2019-10-11 |arxiv=1910.05419 |last2=Bond |first2=J. Richard |last3=Chapman |first3=Scott |last4=Contreras |first4=Dagoberto |last5=Fich |first5=Michael |last6=Halpern |first6=Mark |last7=Hinshaw |first7=Gary |last8=Hlozek |first8=Renee |last9=Sievers |first9=Jonathan|journal=Canadian Long Range Plan for Astronomy and Astrophysics White Papers |volume=2020 |page=53 |doi=10.5281/zenodo.3827103 |bibcode=2019clrp.2020...53S }}</ref> Astronomers estimate that interstellar dust absorbs up to half of all radiation emitted by stars and galaxies, which is re-emitted in the far-infrared and submillimeter bands, making submillimeter observations key to understanding the universe.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Eales |first=S |date=2025-11-28 |title=A very short history of submillimetre astronomy |url=https://academic.oup.com/astrogeo/article/66/6/6.28/8345886 |journal=Astronomy & Geophysics |language=en |volume=66 |issue=6 |pages=6.28–6.34 |doi=10.1093/astrogeo/ataf066 |issn=1366-8781|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Recent findings have utilized submillimetre astronomy to measure the [[Sunyaev–Zeldovich effect]] in galaxy clusters, proving the existence of hot [[Intracluster medium]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mroczkowski |first1=Tony |last2=Nagai |first2=Daisuke |last3=Basu |first3=Kaustuv |last4=Chluba |first4=Jens |last5=Sayers |first5=Jack |last6=Adam |first6=Rémi |last7=Churazov |first7=Eugene |last8=Crites |first8=Abigail |last9=Di Mascolo |first9=Luca |last10=Eckert |first10=Dominique |last11=Macias-Perez |first11=Juan |last12=Mayet |first12=Frédéric |last13=Perotto |first13=Laurence |last14=Pointecouteau |first14=Etienne |last15=Romero |first15=Charles |date=2019-02-12 |title=Astrophysics with the Spatially and Spectrally Resolved Sunyaev-Zeldovich Effects |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-019-0581-2 |journal=Space Science Reviews |language=en |volume=215 |issue=1 |pages=17 |doi=10.1007/s11214-019-0581-2 |issn=1572-9672|url-access=subscription |hdl=1721.1/131738 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

Submillimetre observations have been used to constrain models of planetary, stellar, and galactic formation and evolution.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Lagache |first1=G. |last2=Cousin |first2=M. |last3=Chatzikos |first3=M. |date=2018-01-01 |title=The [CII] 158 μm line emission in high-redshift galaxies |url=https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2018/01/aa32019-17/aa32019-17.html |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |language=en |volume=609 |pages=A130 |doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201732019  |doi-access=free|arxiv=1711.00798 |bibcode=2018A&A...609A.130L |issn=0004-6361}}</ref> By studying foreground elements of the CMB and environments around [[Supermassive black hole|SMBHs]], submillimetre astronomy can also be used to constrain models of [[quantum gravity]] and to investigate the role of [[gravitational wave]]s and [[Neutrino|relativistic neutrinos]] in the early Universe.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="ReferenceA"/> Notably, the [[Event Horizon Telescope]], which produce the first image of a black hole in 2020 using radio and far-infrared observations, also conducts [[Very-long-baseline interferometry|VLBI]] observations within the submillimeter regime at 870μm.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Raymond |first1=Alexander W. |last2=Doeleman |first2=Sheperd S. |last3=Asada |first3=Keiichi |last4=Blackburn |first4=Lindy |last5=Bower |first5=Geoffrey C. |last6=Bremer |first6=Michael |last7=Broguiere |first7=Dominique |last8=Chen |first8=Ming-Tang |last9=Crew |first9=Geoffrey B. |last10=Dornbusch |first10=Sven |last11=Fish |first11=Vincent L. |last12=García |first12=Roberto |last13=Gentaz |first13=Olivier |last14=Goddi |first14=Ciriaco |last15=Han |first15=Chih-Chiang |date=2024-08-27 |title=First Very Long Baseline Interferometry Detections at 870 μm |journal=The Astronomical Journal |volume=168 |issue=3 |pages=130 |doi=10.3847/1538-3881/ad5bdb |doi-access=free |arxiv=2410.07453 |bibcode=2024AJ....168..130R |issn=0004-6256}}</ref>

{{Short description|Half-instanton solution of Yang–Mills theory}}
A '''meron''' or '''half-instanton''' is a [[Euclidean space|Euclidean space-time]] [[solution (equation)|solution]] of the [[Gauge theory|Yang–Mills field equations]]. It is a singular non-self-dual solution of topological charge 1/2. The [[BPST instanton|instanton]] is believed to be composed of two merons.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Allan |first1=Curtis |title=A Mechanism for Quark Confinement |journal=Physics Letters |date=1977 |volume=66B |issue=4 |pages=375–381 |doi = 10.1016/0370-2693(77)90019-3}}</ref>

A meron can be viewed as a [[Quantum tunnelling|tunneling]] event between two [[Gribov ambiguity|Gribov vacua]].<ref>{{cite journal | last = Actor | first = Alfred | title = Classical solutions of SU(2) Yang-Mills theories | year = 1979 | journal = [[Reviews of Modern Physics]] | volume = 51 | issue = 3 | pages = 461–525 | doi = 10.1103/RevModPhys.51.461 |bibcode = 1979RvMP...51..461A }}</ref> In that picture, the meron is an event which starts from vacuum, then a [[Wu–Yang monopole]] emerges, which then disappears again to leave the vacuum in another Gribov copy.

{{Short description|Retarding force on a body moving in a fluid}}
{{Other uses|Drag (disambiguation){{!}}Drag}}
{{use dmy dates|date=May 2023}}
[[File:Airfoil lift and drag.svg|thumb|right|300px|Drag is the component that is parallel to the flow direction, and lift is defined as the component of the [[aerodynamic force]] that is perpendicular to the flow direction.]]

In [[fluid dynamics]], '''drag''', sometimes referred to as '''fluid resistance''', and also known as '''viscous force''', is a [[force]] acting opposite to the direction of motion of any object moving with respect to a surrounding [[fluid]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Definition of DRAG |url=http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/drag |access-date=2023-05-07 |website=Merriam-Webster}}</ref> This can exist between two fluid layers, or between a fluid and a solid surface. Drag forces tend to decrease fluid velocity relative to the solid object in the fluid's path.

Unlike other resistive forces, drag force depends on velocity.<ref>French (1970), p. 211, Eq. 7-20</ref><ref name="NASAdrag2">{{cite web |title=What is Drag? |url=http://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/k-12/airplane/drag1.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100524003905/http://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-12/airplane/drag1.html |archive-date=2010-05-24 |access-date=2011-10-16}}</ref> Drag force is proportional to the [[relative velocity]] for low-speed flow and is proportional to the velocity squared for high-speed flow. This distinction between low and high-speed flow is measured by the [[Reynolds number]].

{{Short description|Branch of geography}}
{{list to prose|date=January 2018}}
{{Geography sidebar}}

'''Development geography''' is a branch of [[geography]] which refers to the [[standard of living]] and its [[quality of life]] of its human inhabitants. In this context, development is a process of change that affects peoples' lives. It may involve an improvement in the quality of life as perceived by the people undergoing change.<ref>[http://www.hi.com.au/geogglobal1/pdf/global1_2_3.pdf Geography of global interactions] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080528032624/http://www.hi.com.au/geogglobal1/pdf/global1_2_3.pdf |date=2008-05-28 }}</ref> However, development is not always a positive process. [[Gunder Frank]] commented on the global economic forces that lead to the development of underdevelopment. This is covered in his [[dependency theory]].

In development geography, geographers study spatial patterns in development. They try to find by what characteristics they can measure development by looking at [[economic]], [[political]] and [[social]] factors. They seek to understand both the geographical ''causes'' and ''consequences'' of varying development. Studies compare [[MEDCs|More Economically Developed Countries (MEDCs)]] with [[LEDC|Less Economically Developed Countries (LEDCs)]]. Additionally variations within countries are looked at such as the differences between [[Italy|northern]] and southern Italy, the [[Southern Italy|Mezzogiorno]].

{{Short description|none}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2019}}
'''Historical astronomy''' is the science of analysing historic [[astronomy|astronomical]] data. The [[American Astronomical Society]] (AAS), established 1899, states that its Historical Astronomy Division "...shall exist for the purpose of advancing interest in topics relating to the historical nature of astronomy. By historical astronomy we include the [[history of astronomy]]; what has come to be known as [[archaeoastronomy]]; and the application of historical records to modern [[Astrophysics|astrophysical]] problems." [https://web.archive.org/web/20060206232209/http://www.aas.org/had/hadhist.html] Historical and ancient observations are used to track theoretically long-term trends, such as [[Eclipse#Historical record|eclipse patterns]] and the velocity of nebular clouds. [https://web.archive.org/web/20011019220913/http://star-www.dur.ac.uk/~jms/group.html] Conversely, using known and well documented phenomenological activity, historical astronomers apply computer models to verify the validity of ancient observations, as well as [[Astronomical chronology|dating]] such observations and documents which would otherwise be unknown.

{{Short description|Study of the region between the ocean and the land}}
[[File:Varing Osmussaar.JPG|thumb|upright=1.25|Collapsed [[Ordovician]] [[limestone]] bank showing [[coastal erosion]]. NW [[Osmussaar]], [[Estonia]].]]
'''Coastal geography''' is the study of the constantly changing region between the ocean and the land, incorporating both the [[physical geography]] (i.e. coastal [[geomorphology]], [[climatology]] and [[oceanography]]) and the [[human geography]] ([[sociology]] and [[history]]) of the coast. It includes understanding coastal weathering processes, particularly wave action, [[sediment]] movement and weather, and  the ways in which humans interact with the coast.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Ibarra Marinas |first1=D. |last2=Belmonte Serrato |first2=F. |title=Comprendiendo el litoral: Dinámica y procesos |publisher=Editum |year=2017 |isbn=978-84-697-6263-9 |language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Davidson-Arnott |first=R.G.D. |title=Introduction to Coastal Processes and Geomorphology |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-521-69671-5}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Woodroffe |first=C.D. |title=Coasts: Form, Process and Evolution |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2002 |isbn=978-0-521-01183-9}}</ref>

{{Short description|none}}
[[File:Korea physical map.svg|thumb|right|upright=1.6|Topographic map of Korea]]
Korea comprises the [[Korean Peninsula]] (the mainland) and 3,960 nearby islands. The peninsula is located in [[Northeast Asia]], between [[China]] and [[Japan]]. To the northwest, the [[Yalu River]] separates Korea from China and to the northeast, the [[Tumen River]] separates Korea from China and [[Russia]]. The [[Yellow Sea]] lies to the west, the [[East China Sea]] and [[Korea Strait]] to the south, and the [[Sea of Japan]] (East Sea) to the east.<ref>[http://www1.korea-np.co.jp/pk/064th_issue/98100708.htm Korean Map] {{webarchive|url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130723104323/http://www1.korea-np.co.jp/pk/064th_issue/98100708.htm |date= 2013-07-23 }}, The People's Korea, 1998.</ref> Notable islands include [[Jeju Island|Jeju]], [[Ulleungdo|Ulleung]], and the [[Liancourt Rocks]].

At 223,179 km<sup>2</sup>, the area of Korea is similar to the area of the [[United Kingdom]] (244,100 km<sup>2</sup>) or the [[U.S. state]] of [[Minnesota]] (225,171 km<sup>2</sup>). Excluding the islands, the area of the [[Korean Peninsula]] is 220,847 km<sup>2</sup>. The peninsula measures approximately {{cvt|1000|km}} from north to south and {{cvt|300|km}} from east to west.{{sfnp|Kim|1988|p=7}}

{{Short description|Thick line used to connect notes in musical notation}}
[[File:Beamed notes.svg|thumb|right|alt=image with beamed notes|A quaver, a dotted quaver, and a semiquaver, all joined with a primary beam (the semiquaver has a secondary beam)]]
In [[musical notation]], a '''beam''' is a horizontal or diagonal line used to connect multiple consecutive [[Musical note|note]]s (and occasionally rests) to indicate rhythmic grouping. Only [[eighth note]]s (quavers) or [[note value|shorter]] can be beamed. The number of beams is equal to the number of [[note value|flags]] that would be present on an unbeamed note. '''''Beaming''''' refers to the conventions and use of beams. A '''primary beam''' connects a note group unbroken, while a '''secondary beam''' is interrupted or partially broken.

