{{short description|Ecological processes}}
{{about|the spread of non-native organisms in the wild|the process of adopting citizenship of a foreign country|Naturalization}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=March 2024}} 
{{Use British English|date=March 2024}} <!--- Per spelling of the article name --->

'''Naturalisation''' (or '''naturalization''') is the [[ecology|ecological]] process by which a [[species]], [[taxon]], or [[Population (biology)|population]] of [[Introduced species|exotic]]—as opposed to [[Indigenous (ecology)|native]]—origin becomes integrated into a given [[ecosystem]]. Through this process, the organism establishes self-sustaining populations, becoming capable of reproducing, growing, and disseminating spontaneously without continued human assistance.<ref>{{cite web |title=Definitions |url=https://www.invasive.org/alien/bkgd.htm |website=Weeds Gone Wild |access-date=4 May 2023 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20230504221402/https://www.invasive.org/alien/bkgd.htm |archive-date=4 May 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> In some cases, a species’ presence in an ecosystem is so ancient that it becomes impossible to determine with certainty whether it is native or the result of a historical introduction. Such taxa may be regarded as naturalised when their origin cannot be conclusively established.<ref name="Lage">{{cite book |first1=Antoine |last1=Da Lage |first2=Georges |last2=Métailié |title=Dictionnaire de biogéographie végétale |publisher=[[French National Centre for Scientific Research]] |year=2015 |page=47 |isbn=978-2-271-08587-0}}</ref>

Generally, any [[introduced species]] may (in the wild) either go extinct or naturalise in its new environment.<ref>{{cite journal|title = Naturalization of introduced plants is driven by life-form-dependent cultivation biases| date=2024 | doi=10.1111/ddi.13788 | last1=Dong | first1=Bi-Cheng | last2=Yang | first2=Qiang | last3=Kinlock | first3=Nicole L. | last4=Pouteau | first4=Robin | last5=Pyšek | first5=Petr | last6=Weigelt | first6=Patrick | last7=Yu | first7=Fei-Hai | last8=Van Kleunen | first8=Mark | journal=Diversity and Distributions | volume=30 | issue=1 | pages=55–70 | bibcode=2024DivDi..30...55D | doi-access=free }}</ref>

Some populations do not sustain themselves reproductively, but exist because of continued influx from elsewhere. Such a non-sustaining population, or the individuals within it, are said to be [[adventive species|adventive]].<ref name="WHS1999">{{cite book |first1=Warren L. |last1=Wagner |first2=Derral R. |last2=Herbst |first3=Sy H. |last3=Sohmer |title=Manual of the Flowering Plants of Hawaiʻi |edition=Revised |year=1999 |publisher=[[Bishop Museum]] Press |location=[[Honolulu|Hololulu]]}}</ref> [[Plant cultivation|Cultivated]] plants, sometimes called [[nativar]]s, are a major source of adventive populations.

